1
00:00:00,259 --> 00:00:03,942
Heat and flooding threaten cold-chain reliability.

2
00:00:04,064 --> 00:00:09,441
Before committing to a retrofit, investigate what could change your choice.

3
00:00:09,549 --> 00:00:13,441
I’m Jangho Lee, and this is GEONOS.

4
00:00:13,521 --> 00:00:22,027
During the hackathon I built this NYC map workspace, its reproducible evidence pipeline and six distinct Meta model

5
00:00:22,077 --> 00:00:23,329
integrations.

6
00:00:23,423 --> 00:00:29,926
Official datasets, pretrained models and software libraries are existing inputs.

7
00:00:30,034 --> 00:00:36,494
This walkthrough follows two real public records, not verified available properties.

8
00:00:36,898 --> 00:00:41,651
At city scale, a density view keeps the map legible.

9
00:00:41,746 --> 00:00:49,972
Zoom in and the official footprint tiles appear across all five boroughs, independently of the current search results.

10
00:00:50,094 --> 00:00:56,903
The preserved source has 1,082,964 records.

11
00:00:57,012 --> 00:01:08,251
We retain 1,082,923 usable outlines and explicitly exclude 41 invalid or placeholder geometries.

12
00:01:08,332 --> 00:01:16,225
Toggle a contextual layer, read its legend and source, then use recorded roof heights in 3D.

13
00:01:16,541 --> 00:01:19,767
Unknown height never becomes an invented measurement.

14
00:01:20,148 --> 00:01:25,971
You can search an address, a building identification number or a tax-lot number.

15
00:01:26,052 --> 00:01:31,931
A tax lot may contain several buildings, so the returned identity matters.

16
00:01:32,081 --> 00:01:40,085
Filters can narrow official records, while a search of the visible map area returns a bounded page.

17
00:01:40,179 --> 00:01:44,336
These are discovery tools, not a citywide optimum.

18
00:01:44,416 --> 00:01:48,420
An unmatched search leaves an honest empty result.

19
00:01:48,529 --> 00:01:52,983
I’ll return to Smith Street and select the two native building records.

20
00:01:53,314 --> 00:01:56,558
The first selection opens the building details.

21
00:01:56,666 --> 00:02:02,951
The second creates an automatic side-by-side comparison, with A and B marked on the map.

22
00:02:03,087 --> 00:02:08,789
For these Smith Street records, 572 has the larger mapped footprint.

23
00:02:08,870 --> 00:02:13,252
Recorded roof height and construction year are separate facts;

24
00:02:13,370 --> 00:02:17,947
neither establishes interior clear height or structural capacity.

25
00:02:18,055 --> 00:02:21,826
The floor-area row is explicitly tax-lot-wide.

26
00:02:21,935 --> 00:02:28,636
Sources retain native identifiers, and selected places stay available while the search changes.

27
00:02:29,009 --> 00:02:37,732
Let’s state a condition instead of inventing a score: at least twenty thousand square feet of usable space.

28
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:43,411
Public outlines do not answer that, so both remain unknown.

29
00:02:43,491 --> 00:02:52,979
If I deliberately change the requirement to mapped footprint, 572 meets it and 592 conflicts.

30
00:02:53,059 --> 00:02:59,380
Available electrical capacity is another missing operational fact.

31
00:02:59,488 --> 00:03:05,669
A sourced owner report can be attached with a date, but stays user-reported.

32
00:03:05,764 --> 00:03:09,525
We have no such report here and will not manufacture one.

33
00:03:09,856 --> 00:03:14,731
Both interior points look blank on the published stormwater map.

34
00:03:14,826 --> 00:03:22,979
That is not a safety verdict: DEP’s methodology represents buildings as voids in the surface-water mesh.

35
00:03:23,087 --> 00:03:25,990
Now look outside the outlines.

36
00:03:26,070 --> 00:03:36,090
In the pinned 2050 sea-level scenario, the mapped class of at least one foot touches 572, while the nearest such area is

37
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:40,251
about forty-seven meters from 592.

38
00:03:40,346 --> 00:03:50,401
Both source scenarios use 2.13 inches of rain in one hour; the future scenario adds thirty inches of sea-level rise.

39
00:03:50,496 --> 00:03:57,135
This directs a site survey of entrances, drainage and equipment elevations.

40
00:03:57,229 --> 00:04:00,400
It does not tell us indoor depth or damage.

41
00:04:00,731 --> 00:04:04,774
Climate context answers a different scale of question.

42
00:04:04,883 --> 00:04:11,801
The observed view uses NOAA station records, with daily data summarized into comparable periods.

43
00:04:11,909 --> 00:04:15,049
A station is not a measurement of this building.

44
00:04:15,158 --> 00:04:23,881
The future view keeps the published NPCC regional projections, periods and baseline separate from those observations.

45
00:04:23,990 --> 00:04:29,963
The same regional outlook applies to both sites; it does not rank their resilience.

46
00:04:30,058 --> 00:04:37,490
It motivates questions about required indoor conditions, equipment performance and the intended operating horizon.

47
00:04:37,939 --> 00:04:45,584
For equipment discussions, a common shortcut is to combine the hottest air temperature with the highest dew point.

48
00:04:45,665 --> 00:04:53,115
At JFK in the retained 2025 observations, those peaks occurred at different times.

49
00:04:53,223 --> 00:05:02,313
GEONOS keeps each actual air-temperature and dew-point pair together, with its timestamp, ties, coverage and gaps.

50
00:05:02,407 --> 00:05:07,024
Move through the observed hot period to see both values change.

51
00:05:07,132 --> 00:05:14,222
These airport samples help frame a question; they are not continuous site measurements or engineering design weather.

52
00:05:14,606 --> 00:05:21,047
A separate example at 211 Lombardy Street shows why the reporting boundary matters.

53
00:05:21,169 --> 00:05:29,972
The parent and one child report contain the same annual site-energy total, but their reported floor areas differ.

54
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:37,507
That reproduces energy intensities of 66.7 and 127.6.

55
00:05:37,615 --> 00:05:40,529
The other child’s energy is missing.

56
00:05:40,637 --> 00:05:46,453
We preserve all three records and never add the parent and child totals.

57
00:05:46,561 --> 00:05:54,378
Before calling a building efficient, ask which meters, areas and operating uses each report covers.

58
00:05:54,759 --> 00:06:00,187
Muse Voice transcribes a short recording into a draft I can edit.

59
00:06:00,295 --> 00:06:08,959
This demonstrated clip is synthetic English speech; it is not a field test of microphone or Korean recognition.

60
00:06:09,054 --> 00:06:12,356
Adding the transcript does not send it.

61
00:06:12,451 --> 00:06:20,865
Muse Spark then chooses bounded official-data operations and carries the question into the investigation.

62
00:06:20,987 --> 00:06:28,179
Source citations come from successful server-side retrieval, not links invented by the model.

63
00:06:28,259 --> 00:06:35,983
Ordinary conversation and a formally evaluated requirement result remain different kinds of output.

64
00:06:36,328 --> 00:06:45,935
For the two-site visual review, the app resolves the native buildings and retrieves official Spring 2024 aerial crops.

65
00:06:46,043 --> 00:06:53,358
SAM 3.1 receives the same images you see and returns roof and tree regions.

66
00:06:53,467 --> 00:06:57,143
The mapped footprint remains a separate reference.

67
00:06:57,237 --> 00:07:03,608
Switch between A and B, hide detections, and compare them with the original image.

68
00:07:03,716 --> 00:07:10,837
The map comparison shows agreement between two representations, not segmentation accuracy.

69
00:07:10,917 --> 00:07:18,191
Neighboring roofs, shadows, leaf-off vegetation and differing source dates all matter.

70
00:07:18,271 --> 00:07:24,067
A missing tree detection remains unknown, not proof that there are no trees.

71
00:07:24,398 --> 00:07:32,635
Once both image analyses complete, Muse Wonder reviews their typed summaries alongside the public evidence.

72
00:07:32,702 --> 00:07:35,467
It does not receive the image pixels.

73
00:07:35,576 --> 00:07:39,498
Its useful output is a short list of survey targets:

74
00:07:39,548 --> 00:07:46,299
roof and equipment, tree context, and the relationship between the dated map and image.

75
00:07:46,408 --> 00:07:52,007
There is also an explicit second-review action for the public-data comparison.

76
00:07:52,101 --> 00:07:55,075
Neither review approves the retrofit.

77
00:07:55,155 --> 00:07:58,275
Each asks what finding could alter the next decision.

78
00:07:58,606 --> 00:08:07,345
The historical view retrieves official 2020 and 2024 photographs with matching geographic crop bounds.

79
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:12,124
I choose when to run DINOv2 locally in this browser.

80
00:08:12,218 --> 00:08:21,818
Its pretrained features produce a 16-by-16 comparison grid after each image is resized for inference.

81
00:08:21,941 --> 00:08:27,791
Highlighted windows tell me where to inspect the original photographs.

82
00:08:27,899 --> 00:08:37,305
They do not certify physical change: shadows, season, processing and alignment can also affect the result.

83
00:08:37,372 --> 00:08:44,942
I can move the divider, hide the grid, and prepare a site-visit question without automatically sending it.

84
00:08:45,273 --> 00:08:49,614
Muse Image serves a different purpose: communication.

85
00:08:49,694 --> 00:08:58,255
An explicit request turns the written inspection checks into a conceptual diagram of what is known, what to verify and

86
00:08:58,305 --> 00:08:59,771
what could change.

87
00:08:59,866 --> 00:09:04,304
It does not edit the aerial image or generate replacement evidence.

88
00:09:04,413 --> 00:09:11,906
The visible label stays outside the picture so it cannot be mistaken for a site photograph or engineering plan.

89
00:09:12,001 --> 00:09:19,564
Source references remain attached to the written brief; the generated pixels are not validated measurements.

90
00:09:19,884 --> 00:09:26,813
Export the selected evidence and the explicit conditions, including what remains unknown.

91
00:09:26,907 --> 00:09:33,489
The methods and visual evidence guide make the source boundaries inspectable later.

92
00:09:33,597 --> 00:09:37,067
Some benign requests still trigger Meta’s filter;

93
00:09:37,208 --> 00:09:44,540
GEONOS preserves that refusal and, where supported, separately labels a public-data brief.

94
00:09:44,648 --> 00:09:47,771
It never disguises that as model output.

95
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:51,558
The result of this investigation is a concrete next step:

96
00:09:51,782 --> 00:09:59,822
obtain plans, inspect drainage and verify usable space and power before committing to either building.
